8006	Assessment of selected pesticide contamination of surface and ground water samples from an agrarian settlement in Kuje area council, Abuja, Nigeria	Joshua OLU, Hajara Oyiza YUSUF, Fatima DANTANKO, Johnson Mathew HILILI	\N	2022-06-29		3833	<jats:p>This study aimed at evaluating the presence and levels of some selected pesticides in both surface and ground water in an agrarian settlement in Kuje area council, Abuja, Nigeria. Samples of water were collected from three areas in Kuje, Abuja, Nigeria namely: Gaube, Kasada and Pasali. The water samples were obtained from borehole, well and stream sources in triplicate. Dissolved oxygen, electrical conductivity, pH, total dissolved solids, and turbidity were the physicochemical parameters determined in the water samples. Five (5) pesticides namely: Lambda-cyhalothrin, Chlorpyrifos, Glyphosate, Dichlorvos and Cypermethrin were investigated using highperformance liquid chromatography-diode array detector (HPLC-DAD). The findings from the study show that pH values were between 5.70±0.30 and 7.82±0.12, electrical conductivity ranged from 61.31±38.41 to 242.81±1.40 μS/cm, dissolved oxygen 5.58±0.69 to 6.40±2.15 mg/L and turbidity values ranged from 1.42±0.51 to 143.11±11.03 NTU. Lambda-cyhalothrin levels were from 0.06±0.00 to 80.30±138.31μg/L, Chlorpyrifos ranged from 139.99±69.07 to 243.49±197.94 μg/L, the concentrations of Dichlorvos were found to be between 86.65±667.12 and 131.22±3.20 μg/L, Glyphosate and Cypermethrin were not detected in any of the samples tested. Lambda-cyhalothrin correlated positively with Dichlorvos (0.721) and Chlorpyrifos (0.951) in stream water sample from Pasali at p&lt;0.05. The concentrations of the three pesticides exceeded the permissible limit except for the Lambda-cyhalothrin in stream water sample from Pasali and this calls for worry because of the potential adverse effects these could cause the residents of Kuje that continuously use and consume water from these sources.</jats:p>	journal-article	1	10.36108/esj/2202.10.0140	Lujosh Ventures Limited	No license URL	No publisher location	0	\N	61	46	\N		https://doi.org/10.36108/esj/2202.10.0140	\N
8007	Modeling of Groundwater Quality Near a Dumpsite Using Principal Component Analysis of Physico-Chemical Parameters	Sochi Otisi ANAGA, Helen NOBLE-OKEREKE, Chizoba Henrietta UNAEZE, Hajara Oyiza YUSUF	\N	2023-12-01		3834	<jats:p>The aim of this study was to determine water quality and develop a predictive model using principal component analysis (PCA) based on physico-chemical characteristics of groundwater sources around a dumpsite. Twelve (12) water samples were collected from groundwater sources around the Solous dumpsite situated in Alimosho Local Government area of Lagos State, Nigeria. Some of the physico-chemical parameters determined in the samples include pH, nitrate content, total hardness, cadmium, iron, lead, manganese, and zinc contents. These were used to determine the water quality index and model using the PCA. The water analysis showed pH levels ranging from 6.15 to 8.55, nitrate concentrations between 1 and 40 mg/L, and total hardness from 17.8 to 720.0 ppm. The concentrations of metals detected included manganese (0.01–0.04 mg/L), zinc (0.05–0.13 mg/L), iron (1.40–12.30 mg/L), lead (0.50–1.20 mg/L), and cadmium (0.03–0.08 mg/L) across all samples. For each of the 12 water samples, the Water Quality Index (WQI) varied between 2636.08 and 3752.60. The PCA revealed that whereas pH (0.865), total hardness (0.741), nitrate (0.816), and manganese (0.770) contents were the significant variables affecting the quality of the water samples within the study region, zinc had the least overall influence on the quality of the various water samples. In conclusion, all of the water samples had a water quality index greater than 300, meaning they were unfit for household (or drinking) use. The pH, total hardness, nitrate and manganese were the four most important variables while zinc was the least variable that influenced the quality of the water samples in this study.</jats:p>	journal-article	0	10.36108/esj/3202.20.0160	Lujosh Ventures Limited	https://esj.com.ng/open-access/	No publisher location	77	2814-1024	89	73	\N		https://doi.org/10.36108/esj/3202.20.0160	\N
